Pre-release review 2026-09-29. A decision for the maintainer, not a bug against the SPEC: SPEC section 7 step 5a says "healthy? -> Running, else deploy", and the code does exactly that.

Consequence: once a Comb is Running, reconcile applies nothing as long as the set of resources is the same. A new image tag in the Comb class, a changed spec.config that the manifests render from, a replica count raised in the manifest -- none of it reaches the cluster until the Comb happens to become unhealthy or the manager calls deploy itself. status.observedGeneration is recorded but never compared. The POD of reconcile names this as open.

For a tool whose job is "deploys itself" this is the first thing a user will trip over.

Options:
1. Generation: deploy when metadata.generation differs from status.observedGeneration. Cheap, no new state, covers every change made through the custom resource (spec.config, spec.class). Does not cover a new release of the Comb class with the custom resource untouched.
2. Digest: record a digest of the rendered manifests (annotation on the resources, or a status field) and deploy when it differs. Covers both. Needs a canonical serialization and one more field in the status schema.
3. Both: generation as the trigger that costs nothing, digest for class changes.
4. Leave it, document it prominently (README, not only the reconcile POD), and tell managers to call deploy on a change.

Recommendation: 2, or 1 for the first release with 2 on the list. Either is a SPEC change (section 7, and section 6 for a status field).

Maintainer decided 2026-09-29: option 2, a digest per resource. SPEC.md section 7 (flow line 5a, the 'Applied digest' bullet under Deploy details) and section 14 are edited by the orchestrator accordingly and sit uncommitted in the working tree; they belong to this card's commit. Details the orchestrator filled in within that decision, open to the maintainer's veto: annotation key <prefix>applied-digest; a digest-triggered deploy of a healthy Comb ends Pending, not Running; resources the client does not replace once they exist (PersistentVolumeClaim, running or succeeded Job) never trigger a deploy by their digest.

